{
  "description": "MAINTAINER RULING #984, the run-lifetime rule applied to a comprehension body. A queued `every` occurrence runs `once the handler is free` for as long as the main line has not finished (spec/interaction-events.md:241-256), and a comprehension iteration is main-line progress exactly as a loop iteration is. It needs its own fixture because a comprehension body is an EXPRESSION: it never reaches the statement executor, so it does not inherit the per-statement boundary that covers `repeat`, `while`, `if`, `for ... in`, and procedure bodies. Measured before this was pinned: `repeat 3 [ print \"y\" ]` and `for i in [1 2 3] [ print \"y\" ]` each gave the queued occurrence three chances to run, while the equivalent `map i in [1 2 3]` gave it NONE -- three handler firings against six. This fixture makes the three containers agree. A runtime that skips the boundary inside comprehensions prints `a` three times here instead of six, which is the same lost-occurrence defect the rest of this group pins in the containers that DO go through the statement executor.",
